Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
pressure to conform to conventional  ideas about beauty is having dangerous  consequences in china  particularly among women it's leading to  increasing cases of eating disorders  like anorexia  and bulimia a study from 2019 estimated  more than 1.5 million chinese  were suffering from an eating disorder  i'll be talking to someone who's  experienced this  firsthand in a moment but first this  report  knows how to love and take care of her  own body today  but she learned it the hard way the 23  year old still has  vivid memories of the severe eating  disorder that  almost killed her at that time i was  around 28 kilograms  it was shot not long before i was  hospitalized  this was when i was being hospitalized  john says her self-esteem was low  affected by  extreme beauty standards shared on the  internet  like this one women comparing their  waists to the width of an  a4 sized paper such challenges are  common on chinese social media  they once convinced zhang that  skinniness will bring happiness  which only turned into a nightmare i  felt it could not walk by myself  my legs and feet didn't have any  strength my body couldn't function very  well  and i couldn't even see very clearly my  hair was very fluffy  and i hardly had any hair left i knew  that i was seriously unwell  but i didn't dare go to the doctor  now recovered chang helped organize this  exhibition against body shaming a  phenomenon experts say has skyrocketed  in recent years  this doll plastered with abusive  messages represents the pain and  struggle  many participants used to have  and this self-marriage ceremony a pledge  to cherish themselves for the rest of  their lives  it moved every single one in the room  i had always thought i was an ugly girl  since i was a kid  and thought that people only like  fair-skinned thin girls  and the process of my recovery i feel  that a healthy skin tone  a sturdy body and a strong heart are the  most important things  in this world  this is what zhang keeps reminding  herself every day  that confidence is what makes one  beautiful  john mina from hong kong is stephanie  ung  founder and executive director of body  banter a hong kong-based  non-profit that shares resources and  information on body image and  mental health stephanie welcome this  pursuit of the ideal body shape  are there any specific cultural factors  in china  for instance that are driving it  thank you for having me today um and i  can speak a little bit about  how there might be some cultural  pressures um based on my own experience  and  also in my research that i've learned  about and i think that something that's  really prominent  is that um there is there is a link  between  um the body the way that it looks and  morality  so um the way that we fundamentally are  so as a lot of us are familiar with um  there tends to be a belief that  um individuals who are in smaller bodies  are more disciplined  or more successful and individuals in  larger bodies are seen as  less disciplined or more lazy and this  link seems to be more pronounced in  chinese cultural settings  um that this link between physical  appearance and morality  in the sense that this association is  really fed to us from  a really early age and is constantly  reiterated in our daily lives  and something important to note about  this link is also that  it's really reiterated and reinforced in  mealtime settings particularly  because mealtime stuttering settings are  such crucial sites of interpersonal  connection  in chinese contexts so for example a  common idiom that's used in chinese  culture is that  if you don't finish your bowl of rice  you're going to marry someone who  has pimples all over their face um and  so it is through this kind of cultural  belief that links  um life outcomes with eating behaviors  that create such a complex linkage  between  how we eat how we look um and who we  fundamentally  are and and do these do these linkages  extend to other areas of daily life as  well for example in school  or in your peer group for instance  yeah so um it seems that um for  especially in  familial settings it is pronounced  because um these are the places where  you have this  intergenerational um passing on of um  knowledge  um but it definitely can extend to  school settings as well because these  are  um this is common knowledge common  cultural knowledge that can be  pervasive in all social settings so if  every  child has been fed this kind of belief  then um everyone in the peer group will  reinforce it as well  so yes i would say um that it is  pervasive throughout different  social groups stephanie how much of a  link  is there with social media for instance  and  the ideal version of beauty that we see  in our times  or is it just a case of the traditional  linkages that you were referring to  right now  yeah this is a question that i've  thought a lot about too um which is that  how much of it is  social media and how much of it is um to  do with cultural beliefs or  beliefs that we've been taught by our  family and i think that there is no  um there's no one answer to this because  um with eating disorders and with all  mental illness on  all mental illnesses it's a confluence  of factors it comes from  it depends on the person's individual  temperament as well  so if this person is very um is engaged  engages with social media a lot and  takes these messages to heart  then social media messaging may be more  um prominent  as a risk factor for them but if this  person is more um  feels more accountable to let's say  their family family's expectations  um or um or other kinds of expectations  that they receive  um then they would be influenced by  those factors more and you know in some  cases and in many cases actually  both of these things will come together  um and reinforce each other  um to create a very complex intricate  kind of pressure  so yeah and stephanie are these  struggles unique to just  women or do men go through these as well  um eating disorders don't discriminate  um they affect anyone  of any um any demographic trait so any  gender  um any socio-demographic um  socioeconomic status  um any um and and um and any background  and any cultural background so um  there's definitely no  um specific um group that is  exclusively um impacted by eating  disorders but um there's  obviously a different set of  manifestations that affect each group so  for example with women um there may be a  greater obsession with  thin ideal with men it may be manifested  in the form  of um of experiencing uh obsession with  the muscular ideal for example  so it manifests differently but everyone  can experience an eating disorder  you spoke about your own experiences  with eating disorders and i wonder if  that can help inform other people about  the warning  signs to watch out for what is it that  people  can watch out for to alert themselves to  what's happening with themselves  or with others  absolutely that's a great question with  warning signs i think  um that there are um a bunch of  different symptoms um  that can can manifest um but i think  something that's very important is  that linkage between morality and eating  behaviors so  a big warning sign is when the person  starts to say things like  you know if i don't eat a certain way  then i'm a bad person or the person  expresses this kind of belief because  when that happens um they can start to  engage in disordered eating behaviors  like  skipping meals like um you know binging  and purging these kinds of behaviors  become  integrated into the person's sense of  identity and then starts becoming a  pervasive pattern of behavior  that becomes a deep part of how they  behave and how they come to see  themselves  um so that is that's kind of a warning  sign when you start to see this sense of  attachment  sense of personal attachment to these  behaviors  stephanie we'll have to leave it there  for the time being but thank you so much  for joining us today with your  very insightful views on this  thank you very much for having me

